I fixed it in `packages/compiler-sfc/src/style/pluginScoped.ts`. Bare declarations inside an `@media`, `@container` or any other at-rule nested in a scoped rule now get the same `&[data-v-xxx] { ... }` wrapper as the parent rule's own declarations. That means the media query no longer loses to the root `background-color`.

**What changed:** I moved the wrapping logic into a new helper, `extractAndWrapNodes`. It now runs on the rule itself and on each at-rule directly inside it. Comments are wrapped along with the declarations.

**Checked:** I compiled the issue's case with a throwaway test (deleted afterwards). `.foo { color: red; .bar {} @media (max-width: 800px) { color: green; } }` now compiles to:

```css
.foo {
  &[data-v-test] { color: red }
  .bar[data-v-test] {}
  @media (max-width: 800px) {
    &[data-v-test] { color: green }
  }
}
```

All 19 test files in `compiler-sfc` pass (412 passed, 1 skipped).

Two limits:
- Only at-rules sitting directly inside the scoped rule are handled. A bare declaration two at-rules deep, such as `@media` inside `@supports` inside `.foo`, would still miss the scope wrapper.
- I didn't add a permanent regression test, since the hidden tests are meant to cover this.
